<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c9/Jose_Rizal_full.jpg" alt="Portrait of José Rizal" class="w-full rounded-xl shadow-md mb-8" loading="lazy" width="800" height="450" />

<p>At seven o'clock on the morning of December 30, 1896, a thirty-five-year-old man stood on the execution grounds of Bagumbayan in Manila, facing a squad of Filipino soldiers in the Spanish Army. Behind them, a backup force of regular Spanish troops stood ready to shoot the executioners if they refused their orders. The surgeon general took the prisoner's pulse. It was normal. Asked for his final words, the man said two: <em>"Consummatum est"</em> — "It is finished." Then he turned his back on the firing squad, a privilege the Spaniards granted him, and refused a blindfold.</p>

<p>His name was José Rizal, and the empire that executed him would be gone within two years. He never fired a shot, never commanded an army, and explicitly opposed violent revolution. What made him so dangerous to Spain was something quieter: <strong>a mind that had taught itself to read at age five, mastered twenty-two languages, and used those languages to write the two novels that woke an entire nation.</strong></p>

<p>Rizal's life is one of history's great arguments for language learning — not as a hobby, but as a form of power. Every language he learned opened a door: to medicine, to science, to allies across Europe, and ultimately to the ideas of freedom that he smuggled back into the Philippines inside the sentences of Spanish novels.</p>

<h2>The Boy of Calamba</h2>

<p>José Protasio Rizal Mercado y Alonso Realonda was born on June 19, 1861, in Calamba, a town on the shore of Laguna de Bay south of Manila, into one of the wealthiest families in the province. His father, Francisco Mercado, was a prosperous farmer who leased land from the Dominican friars. His mother, Teodora Alonso Realonda, was a remarkable woman of Chinese, Spanish, Japanese, and Tagalog ancestry — and she was his first teacher.</p>

<p>Teodora had a deep literary education, an unusual thing for a woman in a colonial outpost. She knew the major Spanish writers, retold their works to her children in ways suited to their age, and made sure the Bible was a constant presence in the home. From her, young José learned to read — and, according to family tradition, he was reading fluently by the age of five. From her he also inherited a habit that would define his life: a love of books so intense that his sisters remembered him reading at the table, in corners, by lamplight, everywhere.</p>

<p>His childhood had its share of sorrow. His favorite sister, Concepción, died in 1865 when José was three; historians later identified that loss as his first encounter with grief. But the household was otherwise warm and intellectually alive. His older brother Paciano, who had lived with the executed Filipino priest José Burgos, acted as an early mentor. The family's wealth bought something rare in the nineteenth-century Philippines: room to learn.</p>

<p>That privilege, though, came with a colonial reality. The Philippines was ruled by Spain, Spanish friars controlled education and land, and a Filipino — no matter how brilliant — was legally a second-class subject. Rizal would spend his whole life testing the limits of that cage.</p>

<h2>Biñan and the Fight Over Spanish</h2>

<p>At age eight, Rizal was sent away from Calamba to the town of Biñan to study under a strict schoolmaster, Justiniano Aquino Cruz, after the death of his private tutor. The school was a nipa house — bamboo and palm leaves — and the methods were brutal by modern standards. Lessons relied on rote memorization, and punishment was frequent. Rizal, who later called Biñan "large and rich, but ugly and dismal," hated it.</p>

<p>The humiliation cut deeper than the discipline. Rizal spoke little Spanish — at home the family spoke Tagalog — and his classmates mocked him for it. One day a classmate's taunt escalated into a challenge to a fight. Rizal, small for his age but wiry and trained, won. The victory earned him a reputation, but the sting of being mocked for his language never fully left him.</p>

<p>That moment is a useful lens on the rest of his life: <strong>Rizal never treated language as an ornament. For him, language was status, defense, and weapon.</strong> The boy who was mocked for imperfect Spanish would later write Spanish so well that Spain's greatest critics praised it — and he would do it by working at the language the way other boys worked at fighting.</p>

<h2>The Schoolboy Who Became "Emperor"</h2>

<p>In 1872, at age eleven, Rizal entered the Ateneo Municipal de Manila, a Jesuit school. The timing was charged: that same year, a mutiny at the Cavite naval yard had led to the execution of three Filipino priests, including the reformer José Burgos, Paciano's mentor. The colonial regime was tightening. The Jesuits who ran the Ateneo, by contrast, ran one of the finest schools in Asia.</p>

<p>His Spanish was still weak. So he did something that would become a lifelong pattern: he attacked the gap directly. During the first year, he took private Spanish lessons at Santa Isabel College during his lunch break, every day. The strategy worked spectacularly. Within two years he had become the class "emperor" — the honorific the Jesuits gave their top student — and he held it repeatedly, topping every subject and collecting gold medals along the way.</p>

<p>He graduated on March 23, 1877, with the highest honors and a Bachelor of Arts. He was fifteen. He had also begun writing: his first known poems date from his early teens, and at eighteen, while studying at the University of Santo Tomas, he entered a literary contest held by the Artistic-Literary Lyceum of Manila. His entry, <em>A la juventud filipina</em> ("To the Filipino Youth"), won first prize. Written in Spanish, it urged his country's young people to raise their heads and build a great future — a startlingly political poem to win a prize in colonial Manila, and a sign of where his languages were taking him.</p>

<blockquote>"Hold high the brow serene, O youth, where now you stand; Let the bright sheen of your grace be seen, fair hope of my fatherland!" — opening of <em>A la juventud filipina</em>, 1879 (translated)</blockquote>

<h2>Madrid: Medicine and Languages at the Same Time</h2>

<p>Rizal's mother was going blind, and in his second year at Santo Tomas he switched from philosophy to medicine, determined to become an ophthalmologist — the one specialty that could save her sight. But he had already outgrown the Philippines. The universities were staffed by friars, the curriculum was stifling, and a Filipino of mixed ancestry could see exactly how far the system would let him rise: not far.</p>

<p>In May 1882, at twenty, he slipped out of the country — his family knew, the authorities did not — and sailed for Europe. He crossed the Suez Canal, landed in Marseille, and took a train to Madrid. In November he enrolled at the Universidad Central de Madrid in two faculties at once: Medicine and Philosophy and Letters.</p>

<p>Then he did something that looks almost reckless: <strong>he took private lessons in French, German, and English on top of his double degree.</strong> He also practiced painting, sculpting, fencing, and pistol shooting — Rizal treated his own education as a full portfolio. Money was scarce; his brother Paciano sent an allowance that never quite stretched, and Rizal sometimes went hungry, skipping baths because they cost a fee. What he did not skip was books. He bought them with every spare centavo, and he studied.</p>

<p>His appetite for languages was not vanity. He was already planning the novels he would write and the research he would do, and he understood that the Europe he needed — its science, its politics, its scholarship — spoke French and German. He was learning the tools before he needed them, which is precisely what separates strategic learners from casual ones.</p>

<h2>Heidelberg: Half the Day German, Half the Day Eyes</h2>

<p>In 1886, after finishing his medical degree, Rizal moved to Germany — first to Berlin, then to Heidelberg, where he completed his specialization in ophthalmology under Professor Otto Becker at the university eye clinic. He worked with the newly invented ophthalmoscope, the instrument Hermann von Helmholtz had created just three decades earlier, and he mastered it so completely that he would later use it to operate on his own mother's cataract.</p>

<p>His letter home from Heidelberg is one of the most famous documents of his life, and for language learners it reads like a perfect lesson plan:</p>

<blockquote>"I spend half of the day in the study of German and the other half, in the diseases of the eye. Twice a week, I go to the bierbraueriei, or beerhall, to speak German with my student friends." — José Rizal, letter from Heidelberg, 1886</blockquote>

<p>Notice what he built: deliberate study in the morning, deliberate study in the afternoon, and <strong>social immersion in the evening</strong> — a structured routine for turning textbook German into real German, using a beer hall as his classroom. He also moved in with a local pastor, Karl Ullmer, in the village of Wilhelmsfeld, living with a German family and speaking the language all day. He was twenty-five.</p>

<p>Heidelberg gave him something else. In the spring of 1887, in the Ullmer household, he wrote the final chapters of his first novel. <em>Noli Me Tángere</em> — "Touch Me Not," from the Latin of John 20:17 — was printed in Berlin that same year. It is the story of a young Filipino who returns from Europe, educated and idealistic, to find his country rotting under friar rule, and who tries to reform it peacefully. It is, among other things, a novel written in Spanish by a man who had been mocked as a child for not speaking Spanish well enough.</p>

<h2>The Twenty-Two Languages</h2>

<p>How many languages did Rizal actually speak? His German friend and collaborator, the ethnologist Adolf Bernhard Meyer, called his abilities "stupendous." Modern biographers, working from his letters, diaries, and the testimony of everyone who knew him, settle on a striking figure: <strong>he was conversant in twenty-two languages.</strong></p>

<p>The documented list includes Arabic, Catalan, Chinese, English, French, German, Greek, Hebrew, Italian, Japanese, Latin, Portuguese, Russian, Sanskrit, and Spanish — plus Tagalog and several other languages of the Philippine archipelago. He learned languages the way other people collect stamps: Japanese during a brief visit to Japan in 1888, Russian during his time in Europe, Hebrew and Sanskrit because he wanted to read the Bible and the classics in the original.</p>

<p>His biographers report a maddening habit: <strong>he switched languages mid-letter.</strong> A single letter to his family might begin in Spanish, veer into French, quote something in German, and finish in Tagalog — which is why translating his correspondence has driven scholars to despair. For Rizal, languages were not separate drawers in a cabinet. They were one continuous tool, and he used whatever piece of it fit the moment.</p>

<p>His masterpiece of translation came in 1886: while in Germany, he translated Friedrich Schiller's play <em>Wilhelm Tell</em> — the drama of a Swiss archer who rebels against Austrian tyranny — into Tagalog, for the education of his nephews. It was the first Tagalog translation of the play, and the choice was not innocent. Schiller's Tell is a story about a people who refuse to be ruled by foreigners. Rizal was smuggling the idea of resistance into his native language in a schoolbook's clothing.</p>

<p>His most famous sentence about language comes from his 1889 letter <em>Sa Mga Kababaihang Taga Malolos</em> ("To the Young Women of Malolos"), written in Tagalog to a group of Filipino women who had dared to ask for education:</p>

<blockquote>"Ang hindi marunong magmahal sa sariling wika ay mahigit pa sa hayop at malansang isda." — "He who does not know how to love his own language is worse than an animal and a putrid fish." — José Rizal, letter to the young women of Malolos, 1889</blockquote>

<h2>The Novels That Moved a Nation</h2>

<p><em>Noli Me Tángere</em> (1887) and its sequel <em>El Filibusterismo</em> ("The Reign of Greed," 1891) were not academic exercises. They were exposés: novels that named the friars' abuses, the courts' corruption, and the colonial system's cruelty, wrapped in plots that Filipinos devoured. The first novel's protagonist, Ibarra, tries reform through peaceful education. The sequel's protagonist, Simoun, returns as a wealthy jeweler plotting violent revolution. Together the books mapped a nation's moral crisis — and they did it in a language the colonizers themselves had to read.</p>

<p>Spain understood the danger immediately. The novels were banned in the Philippines, and Rizal was marked. His greatest European friend, the Austrian scholar Ferdinand Blumentritt, translated <em>Noli Me Tángere</em> into German and wrote the preface to <em>El Filibusterismo</em> — a rare honor, and a sign of how seriously Europe's intellectuals took this Filipino doctor. During Rizal's exile in the southern island of Mindanao, he kept up a stream of correspondence with scientists across Europe, letters in Dutch, French, German, and English that baffled the Spanish censors, who could not read most of what he wrote.</p>

<p>In Dapitan, his place of exile, he did what he always did: he taught. He built a school for local boys, teaching them Spanish — and, in a strikingly modern choice, English as a foreign language — along with agriculture, crafts, and the practical skills to run their own lives. The school's graduates went on to become farmers, honest officials, and in one case a provincial governor.</p>

<h2>The Last Poem and the Last Lesson</h2>

<p>In 1896, revolution broke out in the Philippines. Rizal, who had argued for peaceful reform, was arrested on the way to Cuba — where he had volunteered as a doctor — and shipped back to Manila to stand trial for rebellion. He was convicted by a military court. A day before his execution, his family visited him in Fort Santiago. He told his sisters, in English, that there was something inside the small alcohol stove he was returning to the Pardo de Taveras family. Then he said: "Look in my shoes."</p>

<p>In the stove, wrapped and hidden, was a poem. He had written it in his cell — undated, unsigned, in Spanish — and it was later named <em>Mi último adiós</em>, "My Last Farewell." It is one of the great valedictory poems of the nineteenth century, and its final stanza speaks directly to what he had spent his life believing: that the work of the mind and the pen outlasts the men who wield them.</p>

<blockquote>"Farewell, dear Fatherland, clime of the sun caress'd / Pearl of the Orient seas, our Eden lost! / Gladly now I go to give thee this faded life's best, / And were it brighter, fresher, or more blest, / Still would I give it thee, nor count the cost." — <em>Mi último adiós</em>, 1896 (translated by Charles Derbyshire)</blockquote>

<p>At 7 a.m. on December 30, 1896, he walked to the execution ground at Bagumbayan — the field today known as Luneta, in the heart of Manila, where his monument now stands. The firing squad took aim. He turned to face them, then turned his back at the last instant and refused the blindfold, so that the traitors' bullets would not enter his face. <em>"Consummatum est,"</em> he said. He was thirty-five years old.</p>

<p>He was buried in an unmarked grave in Paco Cemetery, deliberately mismarked by the authorities to discourage pilgrims. His sister Narcisa, who had watched the guards, found the fresh grave and paid the caretaker to place a small marble slab bearing only three letters, reversed: <em>RPJ</em>. Sixteen years later, in 1912, his remains were exhumed and reburied beneath the Rizal Monument at Luneta — the monument Swiss sculptor Richard Kissling had designed, the one that stands there today.</p>

<h2>What Rizal's Life Teaches the Language Learner</h2>

<p>Rizal was not a superhuman. He was a systematic learner with an unusual combination of assets: a literate mother, a family that could (barely) fund his education, and a fury at injustice that gave every hour of study a purpose. His language learning offers lessons that any learner can borrow:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Learn for a mission, not for a certificate.</strong> Rizal studied German to read medicine and science; he studied Hebrew and Sanskrit to read sacred and classical texts in the original; he studied English to reach the Anglophone world. Every language had a job. Learners with a concrete reason — a book, a person, a country, a cause — outlast learners studying "for fluency" in the abstract.</li>
  <li><strong>Structure beats talent.</strong> Half a day of German, half a day of ophthalmology, twice-weekly beer-hall sessions with native students: that is a curriculum. Rizal treated immersion as a scheduled class, not a vibe.</li>
  <li><strong>Use translation as a tool.</strong> His Tagalog <em>Wilhelm Tell</em> was both a gift to his nephews and a training exercise for himself. Translating forces you to confront every gap in your knowledge — grammar, register, idiom — in a way that reading alone never does.</li>
  <li><strong>Speak from day one, even if it means a beer hall.</strong> He went where the language lived and made himself talk. The Heidelberg letter is proof that embarrassing practice — stuttering over German in a brewery — is the fastest road to real fluency.</li>
  <li><strong>Languages compound into power.</strong> Rizal's twenty-two languages connected him to the scholars of Germany, the liberals of Spain, the scientists of Britain, and the censors he could outrun. When the Spanish Empire finally silenced him, the languages had already done their work: the novels were out, the idea was loose, and the nation he wrote for was awake.</li>
</ul>

<p>Two years after his execution, Spain lost the Philippines. The empire he defied was gone; the ideas he wrote in Spanish, German, French, and Tagalog were not. On the monument at Luneta, his own words are carved in bronze: <em>"I want to show to those who deprive people of the right to love of country, that when we know how to sacrifice ourselves for our duties and convictions, death does not matter if one dies for those one loves — for those one loves most dearly."</em></p>

<p>The boy who was mocked for his shaky Spanish, who taught himself during lunch breaks and in beer halls, who translated a play about rebels into his mother tongue for his nephews, ended up proving the single most radical claim a language learner can make: <strong>words are not weaker than empires. Words are how empires fall.</strong></p>

<p>So the next time you open your flashcards and the deck feels endless, remember José Rizal in Heidelberg — half his day on German, half on the diseases of the eye, twice a week at the beer hall with his student friends. He was not gifted. He was deliberate. And he changed the world with sentences.</p>

